  "result": "Fixed. Each save was wiping out the orders already in the file.\n\n**Cause:** `Store.save_order` in `shop/storage.py:17` loads the existing rows, removes any old copy of the order being saved, then appends the new one. The filter was backwards: `if r[\"id\"] == order.id` kept only the rows with the same id as the new order and threw away the rest. When you saved a second order, the first order's row was dropped, so `load()` returned only the latest one.\n\n**Fix:** I changed the condition to `r[\"id\"] != order.id`. Now saving keeps every other order. Saving an order that's already stored still replaces its row instead of adding a duplicate.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_storage.py`, since nothing tested the store before. It has two tests: one checks that two different orders both survive `load()`, and the other checks that saving the same order again replaces its row. The full suite passes, 8 out of 8 (I ran it with `python3`, since `python` isn't on this machine). I didn't run the new tests against the old code to see them fail.",
